This uses coordinate compression on all prefix sums and a Fenwick tree that stores the earliest index seen for each compressed value. For the current prefix, we need an earlier prefix equal to `current - k`. Since this is an exact difference, the tree query checks that one compressed value.

The tree is mainly used to show an indexed prefix-sum method. A hash map is shorter for this exact-match problem, while this version has predictable logarithmic updates.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
    struct FenwickTree {
        vector<int> tree;
        const int INF = 1e9;

        FenwickTree(int size) : tree(size + 1, INF) {}

        void update(int index, int value) {
            for (; index < (int)tree.size(); index += index & -index) {
                tree[index] = min(tree[index], value);
            }
        }

        int query(int index) const {
            int result = INF;
            for (; index > 0; index -= index & -index) {
                result = min(result, tree[index]);
            }
            return result;
        }
    };

public:
    int longestSubarray(vector<int> &nums, int k){
        int n = nums.size();
        vector<long long> prefix(n + 1, 0);

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + nums[i];
        }

        vector<long long> values = prefix;
        sort(values.begin(), values.end());
        values.erase(unique(values.begin(), values.end()), values.end());

        auto position = [&](long long value) {
            return (int)(lower_bound(values.begin(), values.end(), value) - values.begin()) + 1;
        };

        FenwickTree earliestIndex((int)values.size());
        int bestLength = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            long long wanted = prefix[i] - k;
            auto it = lower_bound(values.begin(), values.end(), wanted);

            if (it != values.end() && *it == wanted) {
                int wantedPosition = (int)(it - values.begin()) + 1;
                int startIndex = earliestIndex.query(wantedPosition);
                if (startIndex != 1000000000) {
                    bestLength = max(bestLength, i - startIndex);
                }
            }

            earliestIndex.update(position(prefix[i]), i);
        }

        return bestLength;
    }
};
```

The initial prefix at index `0` represents the sum before the array starts. The code also uses `long long` because many values can be added together. If every value is negative and the target is positive, no match is found and the result stays `0`.

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` because of sorting and Fenwick tree operations.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

A prefix sum turns a subarray sum into a subtraction. If the current prefix is `sum`, an earlier prefix of `sum - k` means the values between those two positions add up to `k`.

The map stores the first position where each prefix sum appeared. Keeping the first position matters because it gives the longest possible subarray for a later matching prefix. If there is no matching range, the method returns `0`.

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

class Solution {
    public int longestSubarray(int[] nums, int k) {
        Map<Long, Integer> firstPosition = new HashMap<>();
        firstPosition.put(0L, -1);

        long prefixSum = 0;
        int bestLength = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            prefixSum += nums[i];
            long neededPrefix = prefixSum - k;

            if (firstPosition.containsKey(neededPrefix)) {
                int startPosition = firstPosition.get(neededPrefix);
                bestLength = Math.max(bestLength, i - startPosition);
            }

            firstPosition.putIfAbsent(prefixSum, i);
        }

        return bestLength;
    }
}
```

The initial entry `(0, -1)` handles a valid subarray that starts at index `0`. For an array such as `[2, -2]` with `k = 5`, no prefix difference matches, so returning `0` is correct.

Time complexity: `O(n)` average.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

A direct version is useful for checking the idea first. For every starting position, keep adding values and update the answer whenever the running sum becomes `k`. Negative values do not cause any special problem here because every possible ending position is checked.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
public:
    int longestSubarray(vector<int> &nums, int k){
        int n = nums.size();
        int bestLength = 0;

        for (int start = 0; start < n; start++) {
            long long currentSum = 0;

            for (int end = start; end < n; end++) {
                currentSum += nums[end];
                if (currentSum == k) {
                    bestLength = max(bestLength, end - start + 1);
                }
            }
        }

        return bestLength;
    }
};
```

For example, with `nums = [1, 2, -1, 2]` and `k = 3`, starting at index `0` gives sums `1`, `3`, `2`, and `4`. The length `2` window is recorded. Starting at index `1` later finds `[2, -1, 2]` with length `3`, so the answer becomes `3`.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the input array.

Instead of storing prefixes in a hash table, this version groups prefix positions after sorting their values. For each prefix ending at position `i`, a starting prefix of `current - k` is needed. The positions for that value are sorted, so binary search finds the latest position before `i`. That gives the longest range ending at `i`.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

class Solution {
    public int longestSubarray(int[] nums, int k) {
        int n = nums.length;
        long[] prefix = new long[n + 1];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + nums[i];
        }

        Map<Long, List<Integer>> positions = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            positions.computeIfAbsent(prefix[i], key -> new ArrayList<>()).add(i);
        }

        int bestLength = 0;

        for (int end = 0; end <= n; end++) {
            long needed = prefix[end] - k;
            List<Integer> starts = positions.get(needed);

            if (starts == null) {
                continue;
            }

            int firstNotAllowed = Collections.binarySearch(starts, end);
            if (firstNotAllowed < 0) {
                firstNotAllowed = -firstNotAllowed - 1;
            }

            if (firstNotAllowed > 0) {
                int start = starts.get(firstNotAllowed - 1);
                bestLength = Math.max(bestLength, end - start);
            }
        }

        return bestLength;
    }
}
```

For `nums = [1, 2, -1, 2]` and `k = 3`, the prefix sums are `[0, 1, 3, 2, 4]`. At prefix `4`, the needed prefix is `1`, and its position is `1`, producing a length of `3`.

The array is left unchanged. Sorting is replaced by the fact that positions are added in increasing index order, so every stored list is already sorted. The empty array naturally returns `0`.

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` in the worst case because of binary searches.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

For this version, prefix sums are processed from left to right. The deque keeps prefix indices whose sums are increasing. A larger old prefix is not useful when a smaller, newer prefix is available, because the newer one gives a longer or equal range for future positions.

When the current prefix minus the front prefix equals `k`, the range is valid. The front index can then be removed because any later ending position would only make that range longer.

```python
from collections import deque

class Solution:
    def longestSubarray(self, nums, k):
        prefix_sums = [0]
        for value in nums:
            prefix_sums.append(prefix_sums[-1] + value)

        candidates = deque()
        best_length = 0

        for current_index, current_sum in enumerate(prefix_sums):
            while candidates and current_sum - prefix_sums[candidates[0]] > k:
                candidates.popleft()

            while candidates and prefix_sums[candidates[-1]] >= current_sum:
                candidates.pop()

            if candidates:
                if current_sum - prefix_sums[candidates[0]] == k:
                    best_length = max(
                        best_length,
                        current_index - candidates[0]
                    )

            if current_sum == k:
                best_length = max(best_length, current_index)

            candidates.append(current_index)

        return best_length
```

A quick check with `nums = [2, -1, 2]` and `k = 3` gives prefix sums `[0, 2, 1, 3]`. At the final prefix, the index `0` gives sum `3`, so the full length `3` is saved.

The empty input returns `0`. Also, the input list is not changed. The prefix array and deque use extra memory.

Time complexity: `O(n)`, since every prefix index enters and leaves the deque at most once.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

